fected. Fine.\"; echo \"10. iar--cycle-complete-p over [turn-start, tracking): sentinel search now only the last turn. c132 discipline already scoped it to model spans; smaller region = strictly safer. ===\""}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[19:05:54] === More edge cases: ===\n4. TOOL-LOOP WAIT without any insert (tool result injected into :data only, no buffer insert?): gptel--display-tool-results DOES insert previews into the buffer via callback (with 'raw). tracking advances. turn-start = post-preview position. So the per-turn region includes the model text + tool previews of THIS turn, not the previous turn's. Good.\n5. STEERING (TSTR): callback inserts steer text raw -> tracking advances -> next WAIT sets turn-start after steer. Fine.\n6. in-place buffers (rewrite): :in-place -- position semantics? rewrite uses its own markers; hook consumers there... gptel-rewrite doesn't use post-response-functions. OK.\n7. Multiple concurrent FSMs (delegates): turn-start is per-INFO (per-FSM), not global. Safe.\n8. The org cleanup-fn leak: real but tiny; document as known behavior change. Actually -- can I avoid it? The org cleanup compares beg == start-marker. I could keep passing position for OTHER consumers and give i.ar a SEPARATE channel: gptel-post-response-functions stays whole-conversation; i.ar switches to reading :turn-start directly via its own advice on the three handlers? NO -- that's worse (more coupling). Simpler: accept the org-cleanup nuance, document it. The temp-buf leak only matters in org-mode interactive tool loops; i.ar cycles run text-mode. ===\n9. iar--cycle-terminal-echo-p reads iar--reqlog-last-tool-specs (per-request) + region text <20 chars. With per-turn regions, an echo-only response region = just that turn -> the post-response DONE branch (dead code today) stays dead; the LIVE pre-tool-call close is unaffected. Fine.\n10. iar--cycle-complete-p over [turn-start, tracking): sentinel search now only the last turn. c132 discipline already scoped it